{"type":"text","timestamp":1792511721885,"sessionID":"ses_ea6165c0bffec0GbYC1A6HUELg","part":{"id":"prt_15a0638eb001eJxRlDNLQc2vEU","messageID":"msg_15a0634a1001dPDlfX27Alvs1q","sessionID":"ses_ea6165c0bffec0GbYC1A6HUELg","type":"text","text":"\n\nDone. **Final verdict: PASS** at the full grade (n≤5, 4,187,856/4,187,856 tests), **work_ratio 0.355** (search level: 0.283).\n\n**The idea (two lines):** Insertions only shorten distances, so re-attach the target under the new edge and propagate decreases with a BFS that relaxes only shortening edges. Deletions of non-tree edges are no-ops (the tree still certifies all distances); a deleted tree edge re-attaches the subtree in BFS order (keep distance iff a surviving in-neighbor one level up exists) and re-reaches the lost remainder from the surviving boundary with a Dijkstra pass.\n\n**Checks used: 4** (limit 8).\n1. PASS, 0.329 — first correct version\n2. PASS, 0.540 — bitset rewrite, regressed (bigint ops cost more than row scans at n≤5); reverted\n3. PASS, 0.283 — children built in the init BFS and maintained incrementally, no rebuilds\n4. `--final` PASS, 0.355 — n≤5, 140k graphs, 837s\n\n**Workspace:** `/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_bfs_s3`\n\nNotes:\n- Correctness was validated locally against a brute-force oracle (400 random graph/sequence trials) before spending checks; two real bugs were caught and fixed this way (stale adjacency masks on no-op updates; phase-2 boundary scan accepting unreachable vertices).\n- The ratio is dominated by init (≈0.67 of offline) plus update work (≈0.24); the remaining gap is Python-level constant overhead, not algorithmic.\n- Scratch files under `evoalgo-scratch/dynbfs` were removed.","time":{"start":1792511719660,"end":1792511721875}}}
